**Integral of a Bivariate Normal Distribution Over a Polygon**

**Example 9.** Let the random variables \(X\) and \(Y\) have a bivariate normal distribution with parameters \(m_{x}\)=5, \(\sigma_{x}\)=2, \(m_{y}\)=9, \(\sigma_{y}\)=4, and \(\rho\)=.5. Find the probability that the point \((X,Y)\) be inside the triangle whose vertices are \(A\)=\((7,8)\), \(B\)=\((9,13)\), and \(C\)=\((2,9)\).

When obtaining the integral of a bivariate normal distribution over a polygon, it is first necessary to use 26.3.22 in order to transform the variates so that one deals with a circular normal distribution. The polygon in the region of the transformed variables is then divided into configurations such that the integral over any selected configuration can be easily obtained. Below are listed some of the most useful configurations.



